    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 100)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    facilities = list(range(3))  # F0..F2\n    customers = list(range(13))  # C0..C12\n\n    # Facility coordinates (x, y)\n    facility_coords = [(7, 41), (44, 42), (68, 90)]\n\n    # Customer coordinates (x, y)\n    customer_coords = [(0, 74), (39, 64), (72, 17), (32, 33), (78, 29), (33, 12), (76, 3), (97, 87), (56, 47), (14, 13), (50, 38), (92, 93), (87, 5)]\n\n    facility_capacities = [180.0, 185.0, 149.0]\n    customer_demands = [14.0, 47.0, 41.0, 18.0, 48.0, 22.0, 29.0, 31.0, 54.0, 28.0, 10.0, 28.0, 42.0]\n    fixed_costs = [305.0, 280.0, 125.0]\n    transport_rate = 0.86  # $/km/unit\n\n    # COMPUTE distance matrix from coordinates (INDUCED COSTS)\n    def euclidean_distance(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    distances = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            distances[(i, j)] = euclidean_distance(facility_coords[i], customer_coords[j])\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Open_F{i}: Binary - whether facility i is opened\n    open_facility = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        open_facility[i] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Open_F{i}\")\n\n    # Ship_F{i}_C{j}: Continuous - units shipped from facility i to customer j\n    ship = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            ship[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Ship_F{i}_C{j}\")\n\n    # Objective: Minimize fixed costs + transport costs\n    # Transport cost = distance * rate * units shipped\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i] * open_facility[i] for i in facilities)\n    obj += quicksum(\n        distances[(i, j)] * transport_rate * ship[(i, j)]\n        for i in facilities for j in customers\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction constraints\n    for j in customers:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for i in facilities) >= customer_demands[j],\n            name=f\"Demand_C{j}\"\n        )\n\n    # Capacity constraints (can only ship if facility is open)\n    for i in facilities:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for j in customers) <= facility_capacities[i] * open_facility[i],\n            name=f\"Capacity_F{i}\"\n        )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Warehouse location decision and customer fulfillment plan\n\nWe need to decide which of our three candidate warehouse locations to open and how to serve thirteen customer locations from the sites we choose. Our objective is to meet every customer\u2019s needs at the lowest total cost, balancing the one-time expense of opening facilities with the ongoing cost of moving product.\n\nThe candidate warehouse map positions are listed in Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (7, 41)\n  - F1: (44, 42)\n  - F2: (68, 90), and the customer map positions are in Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (0, 74)\n  - C1: (39, 64)\n  - C2: (72, 17)\n  - C3: (32, 33)\n  - C4: (78, 29)\n  - C5: (33, 12)\n  - C6: (76, 3)\n  - C7: (97, 87)\n  - C8: (56, 47)\n  - C9: (14, 13)\n  - C10: (50, 38)\n  - C11: (92, 93)\n  - C12: (87, 5). Opening any warehouse triggers a fixed cost per site as shown in [305.0, 280.0, 125.0]. Freight spend will depend on how far products must travel and how much we ship, at 0.86. In practical terms, placing inventory closer to clusters of demand should reduce transport spend, but doing so may require opening additional sites.\n\nEach warehouse has a finite throughput; available capacity by site is provided in [180.0, 185.0, 149.0]. Each customer has a required volume that must be fully met; those requirements are captured in [14.0, 47.0, 41.0, 18.0, 48.0, 22.0, 29.0, 31.0, 54.0, 28.0, 10.0, 28.0, 42.0]. A warehouse can only ship if we choose to open it; unopened sites will not serve customers.\n\nDecisions required:\n- Which of the candidate warehouses to open, considering the trade\u2011off between fixed opening costs and the freight savings from being closer to customers.\n- How to allocate customer orders to the open warehouses so that all demand is satisfied without exceeding any site\u2019s capacity.\n\nSuccess will be measured by minimizing the total cost of the network\u2014the sum of facility opening costs and shipping costs\u2014while meeting every customer\u2019s demand and staying within the limits of each open facility. Once we align on these inputs and rules, we will evaluate scenarios and recommend the specific sites to open and the customer-to-warehouse shipping plan that best meets our cost and service goals.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Decision framework for which warehouses to open and how to serve our customers\n\nWe\u2019re at a decision point on where to open warehouses and how to move product to our customer locations. The aim is straightforward: serve every order reliably at the lowest all\u2011in cost once we account for both the upfront cost to open sites and the ongoing cost to move goods.\n\nHere\u2019s the information we\u2019ll use to frame the decision. The candidate warehouse locations are provided as map coordinates in {FACILITY_COORDINATES}, and our customer delivery points are provided as map coordinates in {CUSTOMER_COORDINATES}. Opening any warehouse incurs a fixed investment captured in {FIXED_COSTS}. Each site has a finite throughput; the available capacities are in {FACILITY_CAPACITIES}. On the demand side, every customer has a required volume that must be satisfied; those quantities are in {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}. Shipping spend is driven by how far a facility is from a customer and how much we ship, at a rate of {TRANSPORT_RATE}.\n\nKey business rules:\n- A warehouse can only ship if we decide to open it.\n- Each customer\u2019s demand must be fully satisfied.\n- No warehouse can be assigned more volume than its capacity.\n\nThe trade\u2011offs are intuitive. Opening more sites can shorten average haul distance and improve responsiveness, but increases fixed costs. Opening fewer sites keeps fixed spend down, but may lengthen hauls and raise transportation costs. Capacity limits and the geographic pattern of demand will determine which customers each feasible site can serve efficiently.\n\nOur goal is to choose the combination of warehouses to open and the shipment plan from open sites to customers that minimizes total cost (facility opening plus shipping) while honoring the business rules above. We will come back with a recommendation that identifies which sites to open, which customers each open site would serve, expected utilization against capacity, and the projected cost impact, along with any relevant sensitivities."
}